The ORDINARY multi-select move, the one `PageBrowserBatchToolbar` sends, and the step that says WHICH of three plausible rules the backend follows. Three arrivals, seeded so that the three candidate orders are mutually distinct:

- source RANK order is `Meetings` (4), `Ledger` (5), `Invoices` (6) — the backend's answer, 1/2/3;
- ID order is `Ledger`, `Meetings`, `Invoices`, because the seed labels run `S5` < `S6` < `S7` while their ranks run the other way;
- the order the op LISTS them in is `Ledger`, `Invoices`, `Meetings`, which is neither of the above, and whose REVERSE — what a handler re-ranking inside its per-block loop produces, each arrival prepending over the last — is neither of them either.

So four distinct answers, and only ordering by source rank gives the backend's. A handler that iterates `blockIds` answers `Ledger` at 1 and `Meetings` at 3; one that sorts by id answers `Ledger`, `Meetings`, `Invoices`; the pre-fix in-loop re-rank answers `Meetings`, `Invoices`, `Ledger`. The list-order arm is the live regression: the fix only moved the ordering out of the per-block loop into a single pass after it, so collapsing that pass back into the loop, or simply trusting the caller's order, is what this step exists to stop.

Destination is the FIRST space, which has taken no move yet, so all three arrivals still outrank every resident and land ahead of `Work` and `Roadmap`. This op runs BEFORE the single-block move because the arrivals must be a SUFFIX of the source group: `move_blocks_to_space` never reprojects the space a block leaves, so taking one out of the MIDDLE strands every later sibling's `blocks.position` above its dense rank in the source doc, and the #891 parity guard fails the fixture on it (#5100). `Meetings`/`Ledger`/`Invoices` hold the last three seed ranks, and once they are gone `Drafts` holds the last.
